A||||16||Spektyr||Where to get a 350 SBC?  (already searched)||||5||
Z||000000||Spektyr||09-02-2001||04:45 PM||||Okay, I did a dozen searches for a thread like this and didn't find one.  If I missed it I apologize.<P>We've had debates over all kinds of motors considering power, cost, weight, sound, difficulty, and probably even which is most likely to get a member of the opposite sex interested in you.<P>I've thought long and hard on this and I've decided that given my wants, needs and money the SBC is the motor for me.  (Unless someone out there has a dirt cheap low miles 3800SC II for me.  Didn't think so.)<P>So now comes the next most difficult decision for me.  Where do I get the SBC?<P>Goals:  minimum of 300hp, maximum of 350.  Torque in the broadest curve possible without forking over mucho cash.  The cheaper the better, no more than $2k, preferably closer to 1500.  Less than that means the Fiero's on the road that much sooner.<P>I'm primarily looking into crate motors, or those with very low miles.  I can build one, and probably do it pretty cheap, but I'd rather let a pro do that and save me the massive amount of time it takes.<P>Also, I'm willing to sacrifice some hps to save some money and get a better bottom end.  Heads, intake, etc can be upgraded later, and without pulling the motor.  Carbed or injected don't make a lot of difference, but cost does, and I don't need easy cold weather starting as this car won't be winter driven.<P>In a nutshell I'm looking for a strong, tough workhorse of a motor that can take plenty of intelligent flogging, provide me with the modest horsepower I want and not require me selling any vital organs.  I'm looking to buy it in at least short block form if not long block.  Decent mileage would be a plus, but if I was after mileage I wouldn't be after a V8 anyway.<P>So where's the good places for me to look?<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||5
Z||000001||pontiacs4me||09-02-2001||06:04 PM||||Check the eagle man.  Parts and accessories section.  Theres always a SBC in there for sale.  I wish I could talk you out of a small block, but you gotta do what you gotta do.  It will be a hellava machine, but Ive decided on a 1988-1990 NA 3800.  After the engine is built and everything Im putting in a T3/T4 hybrid.  Ive decided to build my own headers, y pipe and turbo kit.  I can do anything, I am superman.||||reg||1
Z||000002||artherd||09-02-2001||08:38 PM||||Well, you will get a much better engine if you rebuild one (if you have machine shop access  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> yourself.  Start with a blown (headgasket pref) Corvette LT1 for $200, then bore everything, etc  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>You could put in better cams, and fordged internals for your $2k motor price (be sure to include another $3k for the kit!!)<P>Carb-vs-efi?  You'll pay for the slight additional cost of fuel injecton engines in a year at most with fuel savings.  Plus it will always run, and not stink all icky and stuff.<P>V8 Fieros rock.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000003||Oreif||09-02-2001||08:47 PM||||We were at Archie's yesterday. If I remember correctly, for $2100 you can get a 350 H.O. brand new crate engine from Chevy. It puts out 330hp. Just need the manifold, distributor, and carb. Archie recommends the Edelbrock manifold and 750cfm carb.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ray b||09-02-2001||08:56 PM||||buy a HOT ROD mag it has tons of adds for parts to compleat motors.<BR>or look in bargan trader type mags<BR>or call the junk yards<BR>or ask around at drag strips<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000005||BlackDragon||09-02-2001||09:28 PM||||arth, where do you get a lt-1 for that price? i understand that its blown, but the best i have found was an L-89 with 160000 miles on it for $2000. of course that also comes with tpi, and a computer    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">     but i would rather buy just the engine for less, and worry about tpi later. less expensive<P>------------------<BR>im looking for a inexpensive black standard fastback. does not have to run. does not have to look good. only the mid section needs to be dent free. the fenders,bumpers, dont have to be in good cond. faded paint is fine. anyone who has info, email me. grey interior would be nice but beggars cant be choosers||||reg||1
Z||000006||Archie||09-02-2001||09:43 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>We were at Archie's yesterday. If I remember correctly, for $2100 you can get a 350 H.O. brand new crate engine from Chevy. It puts out 330hp. Just need the manifold, distributor, and carb. Archie recommends the Edelbrock manifold and 750cfm carb.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually I think this engine is about the best value in new crate engines you can get right now. BUT this is not the only good engine available that will fit in the Fiero. The engine is called the 350 H.O., it is 330 HP and has the new Fast-Burn heads... this engine is great. You can check it out at your dealer or online. The engine is available at your local Chevy dealer in the $2100-2300 price range. The Heads alone are better than most aftermarket heads that sell for $1K. It also has the roller cam revs fast like the good old 327's did.<P>Please note that this engine uses a special intake manifold and the TPI style fuel injection cannot be used on this engine.<P>There are a lot of other engine choices, for example read Archism #9 & #10 on this web page <A HREF="http://home.v8archie.com/archism.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://home.v8archie.com/archism.htm</A> <P>Hope this helps'<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000007||Archie||09-02-2001||09:53 PM||||I have to add one more thing, if you don't mind.<P>When discussing LT-1 (LT1) engines, It's easier if we use the proper terminology.<P>Back in 1970 or so Chevy had an engine in the Corvette that was called the LT-1 (&lt;~~note the hyphen). That engine was a 4 bolt, flat tappet cam, high compression, Carburated engine that put out around 400 Brake HP.<P>In 1992 GM revived the moniker in the 'Vette with the LT1 (&lt;~~no hyphen). The new LT1 is also about 400 Brake HP but it has SPI injection, with a roller cam & internally driven Waterpump with "reverse-flow" cooling system.<P>I'm not gonna say which engine is better, I just want to say that these are 2 totally different engines from totally different times, yet they will both go into a Fiero.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000008||LS1swap||09-02-2001||10:56 PM||||check out <A HREF="http://www.paceparts.com" TARGET=_blank>www.paceparts.com</A>  has every GM crate engine to choose from. like siad above 350 HO long block $2,095 or complete carb to pan $3,095 one year warranty on parts. not sure about shipping though.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Philphine||09-02-2001||11:11 PM||||i haven't looked this week but last week someone had an lt1 out of a '96 camaro with harness for $800. don't know what kind of shape it was in.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>you can't win. you can't break even and you can't get out of the game.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Spektyr||09-03-2001||06:12 PM||||Any other suggestions?  There must be more places than this that do good work for the cost.<P>Heck, anyone got a 350 they want to sell?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Archie||09-03-2001||06:43 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Spektyr:<BR><B>Any other suggestions?  There must be more places than this that do good work for the cost.<P>Heck, anyone got a 350 they want to sell?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>All across the country there must be a million good reputable shops that can build you a good or great SBC engine. When I was located in the South Bend, Indiana area (small town) there were no less than 15 shops that were capable of building you a SBC engine to be proud of. Each and every city, town or Village accross the country should have capable engine rebuilders. I just looked real quickly in the NW Suburbs area (Chicago area) phone book and easily found 30 shops that build SBC engines. Additionally several national sources are available. Such as Summit, Jeg's and Jasper (just to name a few). On the Internet it is anyone's guess how many engine sources there are. I just searched "Engine Rebuilder" on Netscape and got 1263 hits.<P>Archie ||||reg||1
Z||000012||pherder||09-03-2001||08:48 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Spektyr:<BR><B>Any other suggestions?  There must be more places than this that do good work for the cost.<P>Heck, anyone got a 350 they want to sell?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>How about a 305 TPI engine with the tpi setup, wiring harness (5 speed), and ECM (needs a chip).  It is a 85 engine (2 piece rear seal) so Archie's basic kit will work without having to get the optional late model flywheel.  I have enough parts and pieces to build <B>MY</B> 350 TPI engine so this tpi engine can go.  Will take $700 AND I am just north of Wichita, in Sedgwick, KS<P>Paul<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000013||Songman||09-03-2001||09:04 PM||||I know everyone gets tired of seeing me post this, but click on the link in my sig. This takes you to the TN Fiero page where you will find a link to e-mail Keith. He sells Grooms engines that come with an 18 month/unlimited mileage warranty and he can beat just about anybodys prices. He used to work for Grooms and he sold all of the engines to Ed Parks that he puts in Fieros. Of course, he can get more than just Fiero engines...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Alex4mula||09-04-2001||08:40 AM||||I'm currently researching this topic extensively. In my local Bargain Trader there are many machine shops ads. One in particular has 350 long blocks for $595 with 1 yr warranty. I think that's a good price but... will need to check which heads and cam it has. For 300HP+ you will need the right heads with the right cam from the start.<BR>The crate 350HO for $2K+ sounds good but once you add the missing parts (intake, distr, carb, etc.) if new will cost the $3K= of the deluxe crate pakg. Up to now if you can find a good cheap rebuild long block with nice flowing heads and cam, and try to find used accessories (or not fancy like the polished Edelbrock intake I want!) then you may end up with a nice 300HP+ engine for much less than $2K. Please keep us posted of your findings.||||reg||1
Z||000015||JasonS||09-04-2001||10:42 AM||||I got a local machine shop to do a rebuild for me.  He is taking his time, but it will be done right.  I should have it back this week.  Here's what I've got so far: sbc 350 re-build, 10:1 hypereutectic pistons, bored .060 over, rebuilt ported heads with 2.02 intake valves, supposed to have a wild cam in it.  Goal = 350 - 400hp. Total <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">minus intake & carb) $2400  I'll post a breakdown of my whole v-8 conversion when it is complete.<P>Jason||||reg||14
Z||000016||pontiacs4me||09-04-2001||06:26 PM||||Josh,<P>This winter, after the car is paid off, Im going to be building a 3800 for my 87.  You build up your SBC, then one friday night well go up to international and line them up.   All I can say is good luck my friend.  <P>Ryan.||||reg||1
